{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And your offspring whom you beget after them.</b> What Ephraim and Manasseh shall beget in your lifetime — they shall be yours, to call them fathers' houses, like the children of Judah etc." "<b>After the name of their brothers.</b> Like 'concerning the field of the land' — the meaning is <b>together with</b> the name of their brothers they shall be called in their inheritance. Therefore it says, 'Yosef saw to Ephraim sons of the third generation' etc. — that is, even though they were not born in the land of Canaan, since they were born in his lifetime they merited to be called fathers' houses, like Hezron and Hamul and Heber and Malkiel, who were born in the land of Canaan. <b>And your offspring whom you beget.</b> For if you examine the sugya in the chapter 'Yesh nohlin' (There are those who inherit) you will not find after the conclusion what Rashi explained here, for there they raise the question: did the tribes divide, or did the heads of men divide? — and they conclude that the tribes divided. And there they derive: to Rav, his inheritance increased in a different manner."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {1 "The bracketed insertion [עם] appears in the Hebrew text; included as written. The phrase פי׳‎ על [עם] שם means the author glosses 'על' as 'עם'. The sugya referenced is Bava Batra 111b–113a, chapter יש נוחלין."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:09:04.427437480Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}